WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.805
Seeding determines the starting state of many pseudo-random generators.

2
00:00:03.805 --> 00:00:08.372
If the seed is predictable, the generated sequence may also be predictable.

3
00:00:08.372 --> 00:00:15.602
That is why timestamps, process identifiers, counters, small integers, build numbers, configuration constants, and fixed values are not appropriate

4
00:00:15.602 --> 00:00:16.363
security seeds.

5
00:00:16.363 --> 00:00:23.973
They may look variable during normal testing, but they often have far less uncertainty than a security-sensitive design requires.

6
00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:25.876
Repeated seeds create another problem.

7
00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:31.204
A deterministic generator started from the same seed can produce the same sequence again.

8
00:00:31.204 --> 00:00:34.248
In test code, that behavior can be useful.

9
00:00:34.248 --> 00:00:43.000
In production security code, it can create repeated identifiers, repeated token patterns, or values that are easier to reason about than the system

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.761
owner intended.

11
00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:47.947
Trying to combine several weak inputs manually rarely fixes the issue.

12
00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:54.035
Manual entropy assembly is difficult to evaluate, and future maintenance can quietly remove or weaken assumptions.

13
00:00:54.035 --> 00:01:00.124
Security-sensitive systems should rely on trusted entropy sources and CSPRNGs rather than improvising seed material.

14
00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:01.646
Error handling matters too.

15
00:01:01.646 --> 00:01:09.257
If the required source is unavailable or fails, the program should fail explicitly or follow a documented safe failure path.

16
00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:16.106
Silent fallback to a deterministic generator, a fixed seed, or a timestamp seed is a serious design smell.

17
00:01:16.106 --> 00:01:23.717
Treat seed handling like security configuration: review it, centralize it where practical, document the approved behavior, and make weak modes

18
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:26.000
visible before they can reach production.
